[Music]  if you have young children you know the  challenge of looking after them and  trying to work it's a constant  exhausting juggle for a lot of parents  child care is essential and it's just  not back to where it was pre-pandemic a  national survey conducted for the  Canadian child care Federation found  that 36 percent of child care centres  that closed because of kovat 19 might  not reopen at all and 38 percent of home  daycares could remain closed those that  are reopening face a precarious  financial future sixty-eight percent say  they're worse off than they were before  Mike Joel a looks at the rocky road to  recovery of child care every day in  cities and towns across the country kids  are dropped off to start their days it's  about socialization the economy and the  collective sanity of parents everywhere  oh she absolutely loves it Beatrice had  only been in daycare three months when  everything shut down for her mama  Julia Mackenzie it meant balancing the  work she had just returned to with the  needs of a toddler she now had to care  for 24 hours a day so maybe about three  weeks ago we both kind of said okay we  don't know how much longer we could do  this we need some help  do we need to hire a nanny what's what  are our options here there's a lot of  guilt and familiarity to parents in  those words with no familial support  because of kovat 19 Beatrice spent more  time in front of the TV than normal and  no time interacting with other kids  after three months she's become  distrustful of others and in the street  when people walk too close to her I  noticed she clings to me so that's been  a huge concern actually it scares me a  little bit I don't want her to become  you're full and I don't want her to  learn that it's not okay to to play with  people but in the new reality Beatrice  who returns to daycare on Monday is one  of the lucky ones because there are  fewer childcare spots available BC has  implemented strict rules when it comes  to contact and distancing with children  but has an assigned a number for how  large groups can be Manitoba recently  removed its size restrictions Quebec  will follow suit in July but Alberta  Saskatchewan Ontario New Brunswick and  Nova Scotia have imposed strict limits  to keep groups small which has  effectively shrunk the system's capacity  a Toronto city councilor estimates there  will be 56,000 fewer spots in Toronto  alone Amy O'Neill of treetop childcare  says her Center can't opened under the  current limit of 10 people per room  including teachers so we usually have  approximately 155 but we're licensed for  many more with the new reopening  guidelines I could accommodate 24  financially she says they'd be bankrupt  within two months and on a personal note  she wants nothing to do with the  lotteries other centers have used to  assign the spots they do have that's not  our business to decide who is eligible  and who is not O'Neill is waiting for  direction from the government until then  she and the families that rely on her  are in limbo in Peterborough the staff  of nearly a dozen daycares decided the  situation was untenable and recently  took to the streets to protest Prime  Minister Justin Trudeau did announce an  aid package this past week stating what  some critics say was pretty obvious well  child care is going to require more  spaces more rooms more space in  different modifications which will cost  more money so we want to be there to  help that child care advocates are  asking that 2.5 billion of the 14  billion dollars pledged be earmarked to  help address critical issues beyond that  Carolyn ferns of the Ontario Coalition  for better childcare says the guidelines  as a whole are far too restrictive and  Ontario children can be picked up by  their teachers  provided a blanket is between them and  that blanket can't be used again I know  that from early childhood educators that  I have spoken with that's one of their  top concerns they don't want to be  running a childcare program with  children in chalk squares and even more  perplexing is this nugget from the  provinces 20 pages of guidelines  children should not do activities that  involve sharing toys and they should  avoid singing that's just not a  realistic expectation considering the  push for small groups home daycares  would seem to be a logical alternative  over a half of we watch as thousand  homes continue to operate during the  pandemic and they have a list of new  daycares ready to go but the first aid  courses they need for certification are  still closed  we could have 120 spaces available  pretty quickly but we can't get the  certification and we've asked if they  could do small group sizes like  one-on-ones or maybe three providers in  a room a little social distance then we  can do the whole thing how do we work  around this and we just can't seem to  get anywhere it's more red tape more  rules more uncertainty for an essential  service I don't know they keep saying  it's based on medicine and evidence and  I wonder if it's more political putting  excessive guidelines around children  isn't practical  argues dr. Anna Banerjee because they're  considered low-risk to a virus that is  unlikely to be completely eradicated I  don't think we can contain it I think  slow it down but I think until we have a  vaccine or until there's a current  immunity Co it's gonna be around for a  while these next few months will be  critical for child care providers to  prove they can handle outbreaks and  provide a reprieve for parents it's  having a huge impact on people as well  you can only hold back the floodgates  for so long

